---
import Divider from "../../../components/ui/Divider.astro";
import Layout from "../../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components";
import { Code as CodeSnippet } from "astro:components";

//#region CodeSnippets
const FirstProgram = `io.write("What is your name? ")
local name = io.read()
print("Nice to meet you " .. name)`;

const FirstProgramOut = `What is your name? Ada [Enter]
Nice to meet you Ada`;

const GameProgram = `function love.draw()
    love.graphics.print("Hello World!", 400, 300)
end

function love.keypressed(key)
    if key == "escape" then
        love.event.quit()
    end
end`;

const RuntimeErrorCode = `local scores = {90, 85, 78}
print("Third score: " .. scores[3])
print("Fourth score: " .. scores[4])`;

const RuntimeErrorOut = `Third score: 78

Error: main.lua:3: attempt to concatenate a nil value

  main.lua:3: in main chunk`;

const SyntaxErrorCode = `local x = 5
if x > 3 then
    print("big")`;

const SyntaxErrorOut = `Error: Syntax error: main.lua:4: 'end' expected (to close 'if' at line 2) near '<eof>'`;
//#endregion
---

<Layout>
  <div class="container my-4">
    <div class="row justify-content-center">
      <div class="col-sm-12 col-md-10">
        <a href="/courses/ap-csp-lua/" class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-light mb-3">
          <i class="si-arrow-left"></i> Back
        </a>

        <h1>Setting Up Your Workspace</h1>
        <Divider variant="8" classes="mb-5" />

        <section id="what-you-need">
          <h2>What You Need</h2>

          <p>
            Two things: <b class="text-sn-theme">VS Code</b> and{" "}
            <b class="text-sn-theme">LÖVE</b>. On a lab computer both are
            already installed and you can skip straight to the next section.
          </p>

          <p>
            LÖVE is doing two jobs at once. It runs your Lua code, and it is
            also the drawing library this course uses. <b>That is why there is
            nothing else to install</b> &mdash; no separate Lua, no package
            manager, and nothing in this course needs an internet connection
            once you have the workspace folder.
          </p>

          <p class="text-secondary">
            Working on your own computer? Install VS Code from{" "}
            <a href="https://code.visualstudio.com/" target="_blank"
              >code.visualstudio.com</a
            > and LÖVE from <a href="https://love2d.org/" target="_blank"
              >love2d.org</a
            >. Get <b>LÖVE 11.5</b> if you are offered a choice &mdash; error
            messages change wording between versions, and that is the one this
            course is written against. On a Mac, install it by running{" "}
            <CodeSnippet code="brew install --cask love" lang="txt" inline />{" "}
            if you can, because that puts LÖVE somewhere the Run button can
            find it.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <section id="get-the-workspace">
          <h2>Get the Workspace</h2>

          <ol>
            <li class="mb-2">
              <b>Download it.</b>{" "}
              <a href="/workspaces/lua-workspace.zip" class="text-sn-theme"
                >lua-workspace.zip</a
              >
            </li>
            <li class="mb-2">
              <b>Unzip it.</b> Right-click the file and choose <i
                >Extract All</i
              >. You want the folder somewhere you can find again &mdash; your
              Documents folder is fine, your Downloads folder is not.
            </li>
            <li class="mb-2">
              <b>Open the folder in VS Code.</b> <i>File &gt; Open Folder</i>,
              then pick the{" "}
              <CodeSnippet code="lua-workspace" lang="txt" inline /> folder
              itself. Not the folder above it, and not a file inside it.
            </li>
            <li class="mb-2">
              <b>Say yes to the extensions.</b> VS Code will offer to install
              three recommended extensions. Click <i>Install</i>.{" "}
              <b class="text-sn-theme">The Run button does not exist until you
              do</b>, and the offer only appears once.
            </li>
          </ol>

          <p class="text-secondary">
            If you missed the extension prompt, open the Extensions panel and
            type <CodeSnippet code="@recommended" lang="txt" inline /> in its
            search box. The same three will be listed.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <section id="first-program">
          <h2>Your First Program</h2>

          <p>
            Inside the workspace there are two folders whose names start with{" "}
            <CodeSnippet code="template-" lang="txt" inline />. You never work
            in them. <b class="text-sn-theme">You copy one</b>, rename the copy,
            and work in that. Every activity in this course starts the same way.
          </p>

          <ol>
            <li class="mb-2">
              Right-click <CodeSnippet code="template-console" lang="txt" inline />{" "}
              and choose <i>Copy</i>, then <i>Paste</i>. You get a second folder.
            </li>
            <li class="mb-2">
              Rename it to <CodeSnippet code="1-01-first-run" lang="txt" inline />.
            </li>
            <li class="mb-2">
              Open <CodeSnippet code="main.lua" lang="txt" inline /> inside it.
              It already contains this:
            </li>
          </ol>

          <Code code={FirstProgram} lang="lua" frame="code" title="main.lua" />

          <p>
            Press the <b class="text-sn-theme">Run</b> button &mdash; the ▷ at
            the top right. A terminal panel opens at the bottom, asks for your
            name, and waits. Type it and press Enter:
          </p>

          <Code
            code={FirstProgramOut}
            lang="txt"
            frame="terminal"
            title="Terminal"
            showLineNumbers={false}
          />

          <p>
            That is the whole loop for this course: copy a template, write, press
            Run, read what came out.
          </p>

          <div class="alert alert-warning mt-3">
            <b>Do not edit <CodeSnippet code="conf.lua" lang="txt" inline />.</b>{" "}
            It is the other file in the folder, and it is what makes the Run
            button behave properly &mdash; it decides whether a window opens and
            makes sure your output appears the moment it is printed. Everything
            you write goes in{" "}
            <CodeSnippet code="main.lua" lang="txt" inline />.
          </div>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <section id="two-templates">
          <h2>Two Templates, and They Are Not Interchangeable</h2>

          <p>
            <b class="text-sn-theme">template-console</b> &mdash; a program that
            prints text and reads what you type. No window opens.{" "}
            <CodeSnippet code="main.lua" lang="txt" inline /> is ordinary Lua: it
            runs from the top of the file to the bottom and then the program ends.
          </p>

          <p>
            <b class="text-sn-theme">template-game</b> &mdash; a program that
            opens a window and draws in it. Here{" "}
            <CodeSnippet code="main.lua" lang="txt" inline /> is a set of
            functions that LÖVE calls for you, over and over, many times a
            second:
          </p>

          <Code code={GameProgram} lang="lua" frame="code" title="main.lua" />

          <p>
            <b>You did not write the code that calls those.</b> LÖVE calls{" "}
            <CodeSnippet code="love.draw" lang="lua" inline /> every frame, and{" "}
            <CodeSnippet code="love.keypressed" lang="lua" inline /> whenever a
            key goes down. Think of them as boxes the engine opens: you decide
            what goes inside, and something else decides when to look. We come
            back to what <CodeSnippet code="function" lang="lua" inline /> really
            means later in the quarter, and by then you will have been writing
            them for weeks.
          </p>

          <div class="alert alert-info">
            <b>An activity either draws or reads typed input. Never both.</b>{" "}
            The activity page always tells you which template to copy. Reading
            input while a window is open makes the window sit there doing nothing
            &mdash; which looks broken and is not.
          </div>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <section id="how-run-works">
          <h2>What the Run Button Actually Runs</h2>

          <p>
            <b class="text-sn-theme">Run runs the folder, not the file.</b> It
            looks at whichever file you have open, finds the folder that file is
            sitting in, and runs the{" "}
            <CodeSnippet code="main.lua" lang="txt" inline /> there.
          </p>

          <p>
            Most of the time this is exactly what you want: you can have any file
            in the activity folder open, press Run, and the activity runs. Two
            rules keep it that way.
          </p>

          <ul>
            <li class="mb-2">
              <b>One activity per folder</b>, each with its own{" "}
              <CodeSnippet code="main.lua" lang="txt" inline /> and{" "}
              <CodeSnippet code="conf.lua" lang="txt" inline />. That is what
              copying a template gives you.
            </li>
            <li class="mb-2">
              <b>No folders inside an activity folder.</b> Open a file one level
              down and Run points at the wrong place.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <section id="where-work-goes">
          <h2>Where Your Work Goes</h2>

          <p>
            One folder per activity, sitting next to the two templates, named the
            way the activity page tells you. Nothing else to organize.
          </p>

          <div class="alert alert-info">
            <b>Keep everything you write.</b> In May, the Create Performance Task
            asks you to write about a program you built &mdash; from memory,
            without the code in front of you. The work you keep now is what you
            will have to draw on then.{" "}
            <b class="text-sn-theme">Never edit an old activity to make a new
            one.</b> Copy a template instead. That is the entire reason the
            templates exist.
          </div>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <section id="reading-errors">
          <h2>Reading an Error</h2>

          <p>
            <b class="text-sn-theme">Read the message before you change
            anything.</b> Where the message appears depends on which template
            you copied, so that is the first thing to notice.
          </p>

          <h3 class="mt-4">It started, then stopped</h3>

          <p>A console program that asks for something that is not there:</p>

          <Code
            code={RuntimeErrorCode}
            lang="lua"
            frame="code"
            title="main.lua"
            showLineNumbers={false}
          />
          <Code
            code={RuntimeErrorOut}
            lang="txt"
            frame="terminal"
            title="Terminal"
            showLineNumbers={false}
            class="mt-2"
          />

          <p>
            <b>Notice that the first line printed.</b> The program ran, got to
            line 3, and stopped there. A list with three things in it has no
            third-and-then-some &mdash; there is no{" "}
            <CodeSnippet code="scores[4]" lang="lua" inline />, so there was
            nothing to join onto the text.
          </p>

          <h3 class="mt-4">Nothing runs at all</h3>

          <p>A missing <CodeSnippet code="end" lang="lua" inline />:</p>

          <Code
            code={SyntaxErrorCode}
            lang="lua"
            frame="code"
            title="main.lua"
            showLineNumbers={false}
          />
          <Code
            code={SyntaxErrorOut}
            lang="txt"
            frame="terminal"
            title="Terminal"
            showLineNumbers={false}
            class="mt-2"
          />

          <p>
            <b>Nothing printed</b>, because the program never started. Read this
            one carefully &mdash; it names <i>two</i> lines. Line 4 is where Lua
            gave up looking, and line 2 is where the thing it was looking for
            should have been closed. <b class="text-sn-theme">The mistake is
            almost always at the second number, not the first.</b>
          </p>

          <h3 class="mt-4">A blue screen instead of your game</h3>

          <p>
            When a program that draws hits an error, LÖVE takes over the window
            and fills it with a blue screen showing what went wrong and the line
            it happened on. Read it, <b class="text-sn-theme">press Escape</b> to
            close it, fix the line, run it again.
          </p>

          <p class="text-secondary">
            <b>The tell is whether anything happened first.</b> Output and then a
            stop means the program ran and hit something part way through.
            Nothing at all means it never got started, and that is nearly always
            a missing <CodeSnippet code="end" lang="lua" inline /> or an unclosed
            quote.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <section id="troubleshooting">
          <h2>If Something Goes Wrong</h2>

          <dl>
            <dt class="text-sn-theme mt-3">
              The window is black and nothing is happening
            </dt>
            <dd>
              <b>Your program is waiting for you to type something.</b> Click the
              terminal panel at the bottom and look &mdash; there is a prompt
              sitting there. This is the single most common thing that looks like
              a crash and is not, and it happens when a program both draws and
              reads input, which is why the activities never ask you to do both.
            </dd>

            <dt class="text-sn-theme mt-3">
              A blue screen appeared and I cannot get rid of it
            </dt>
            <dd>
              Press <b>Escape</b>. That closes it and ends the program. The blue
              screen is LÖVE reporting an error rather than anything being stuck,
              so read what it says before you dismiss it &mdash; it names the
              line.
            </dd>

            <dt class="text-sn-theme mt-3">The Run button is not there</dt>
            <dd>
              The Code Runner extension did not install. Open the Extensions
              panel, search{" "}
              <CodeSnippet code="@recommended" lang="txt" inline />, and install
              all three.
            </dd>

            <dt class="text-sn-theme mt-3">
              Something about <CodeSnippet code="lovec" lang="txt" inline /> not
              being recognized
            </dt>
            <dd>
              LÖVE is not installed, or the Run button cannot find it. On your
              own computer this is the most likely thing to go wrong, and it is a
              setup problem rather than a problem with your code. Tell me and
              bring the exact message.
            </dd>

            <dt class="text-sn-theme mt-3">
              Run does nothing, or runs the wrong activity
            </dt>
            <dd>
              Check which file you have open. Run uses the folder that file is
              in, so an open file from a different activity runs{" "}
              <i>that</i> activity. If you put a folder inside your activity
              folder, take it back out.
            </dd>

            <dt class="text-sn-theme mt-3">
              I opened VS Code and everything looks plain
            </dt>
            <dd>
              You opened the wrong folder. VS Code has to be opened at{" "}
              <CodeSnippet code="lua-workspace" lang="txt" inline /> itself, not
              at the folder containing it, or the settings that make any of this
              work are not loaded. Close it and reopen with{" "}
              <i>File &gt; Open Folder</i>.
            </dd>

            <dt class="text-sn-theme mt-3">Something else</dt>
            <dd>
              Tell me, and say what you pressed and what appeared. A setup
              problem is not something you should spend the period on.
            </dd>
          </dl>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <h1
          class="text-center mt-5 text-sn-theme border border-primary slow-flash"
        >
          Ready to Start
        </h1>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
